<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis Light</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1740">1740</a>–<a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis Light was a British trader and naval officer who founded the British settlement on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a> in 1786. He obtained the island from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Abdullah of Kedah on behalf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, implying military protection that the company never gave. He named the island <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> Island and founded George Town as a free port, serving as superintendent until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-light.jpg" alt="Francis Light" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pangalau · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_Cornwallis,_Penang_2023_09.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Light was born in Dallinghoo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>, and baptised on 15 December 1740. Taken in by a relative, William Negus, he was sent to Woodbridge Grammar School in 1747. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> in February 1754 as a surgeon's mate on HMS Mars, later served on HMS Captain and HMS Dragon, and was promoted to midshipman on HMS Arrogant in 1761 before leaving the navy in 1763. In the navy he met James Scott, later his business partner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1765 Light sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where he took command of the Speedwell, a country ship of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> trading firm. For about ten years he was based at Thalang in Siam, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phuket">Phuket</a>, trading there, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>, and learning Malay and Thai. With Martina Rozells he set up a trading post at Kuala Kedah and gained influence with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Kedah. In 1785 his warning of a Burmese attack helped the islanders of Thalang, led by Than Phu Ying Chan and her sister Mook, to repel the invasion.</p><p>Light had proposed a British settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a> as early as 1771. In 1786 he leased the island on behalf of the company for 6,000 Spanish dollars a year and was put in charge as superintendent on 1 July. The island was then described as nearly 107 square miles of jungle with only fifty-eight inhabitants; by 1789 there were about 10,000, and by 1795, 20,000. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>'s lands were invaded and no aid came, the Sultan tried to take back the island in 1790. In 1794 Light recommended a proper system of justice, so that the superintendent would not dispense arbitrary judgement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Light died of malaria on 21 October 1794 and was buried in the Old Protestant Cemetery in George Town. With Martina Rozells he had three daughters and two sons. The elder son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-light">William Light</a>, became the first Surveyor General of South Australia and chose the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a>. A bronze statue erected at Fort Cornwallis in 1936, 150 years after George Town's founding, bears Francis Light's name but was modelled on William's features, as there was no portrait of the father.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francis Light” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1786 Sultan Abdullah Mukarram Shah allowed <b>Francis Light</b> of the British East India Company to occupy Penang, hoping for protection that never came.</blockquote><a href="/state/kedah-sultanate">Kedah Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Seeking a naval and trading base on the eastern side of the Bay of Bengal, the East India Company trader <b>Francis Light</b> negotiated with the Sultan of Kedah, who hoped for protection against Siam.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-of-wales-island">Prince of Wales Island</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dallinghoo, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.15,1.31&amp;year=1775" aria-label="Dallinghoo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4580 363 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francis Light"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5030 633h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5030 633h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.15,1.31&amp;year=1775">What was here in 1775? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
